  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a mixed day. Port Washington took a 29% revenue hit\u2014but that's entirely a capacity story. You ran five groomers instead of your typical seven, and they performed at $480 per groomer, actually *above* your 30-day baseline of $474. The shortfall was bather hours: zero instead of your 5.4-day average. That's a scheduling gap, not a productivity gap. Hicksville stayed flat, with three groomers hitting $470 per groomer\u2014an 8% beat on baseline\u2014so the smaller location is running hot on efficiency. The real concern is Port Washington's retail revenue: down 22% while core grooming was down 30%. That 8-point divergence suggests add-on and retail attachment discipline may have slipped, or the customer mix skewed lighter. Watch that tomorrow when you're fully staffed at seven groomers and 15 bather hours.\n\nMonth-to-date, both stores are outperforming. Port Washington is up 6% absolute and 4.9% after adjusting for calendar\u2014real performance gain. Hicksville is up 14% absolute and *18% after DOW adjustment*\u2014that's significant overperformance and worth understanding. Year-to-date Port Washington is up 44% appointments and revenue compared to last year, which is the headline win. The calendar is favoring you both this month.\n\nOne operational alert: you have 588 customers in your lapse pool across both locations, representing $388k in annual value. That's reactivation capital sitting idle. Hicksville's lapse pool is proportionally larger (53% drifting or lapsed vs Port's 36%), suggesting retention risk at the smaller location. Prioritize outreach there.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Port Washington has 7 groomers and 15 bather hours scheduled today, 6 groomers and 15 bathers tomorrow; next 7 days you have 37 groomer-days and 83 bather hours across both locations combined."
